---
import Guide from "@layouts/Guide.astro";
import { FOR_SERVICES_NAV } from "@data/nav-sections";
const crumbs = [{ name: "Home", href: "/" }, { name: "For services", href: "/for-services" }, { name: "Integration overview", href: "/for-services/integration-overview" }];
---
<Guide title="Integration overview" metaTitle="Verification Integration Overview | identity.org.au" description="Separate the reference demonstration from production verification. Start with claim scope, issuer trust, holder authentication, freshness, status and retention." lede="A useful integration needs accepted evidence, a clear request and a verified result. Source code and illustrative messages are starting points, not a production contract." eyebrow="For services · reference infrastructure" breadcrumbs={crumbs} sectionLabel="For services" sectionNav={FOR_SERVICES_NAV}>
  <div class="prose-service">
    <h2>Begin with the pilot example</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/for-services/pilot-integration">pilot integration page</a> contains a proposed membership data flow and illustrative request/result messages. The <a href="/for-services/demo">interactive demo</a> lets you try consent, refusal and failure outcomes. Neither is a production API or cryptographic verifier.</p>
    <h2>Define what your service needs</h2>
    <p>Specify the claim, purpose and assurance. Decide which issuers have authority to make that claim, what evidence they use and how their credentials expire or are revoked. A tier or score alone is not proof of suitability for your decision.</p>
    <h2>Verify more than a signature</h2>
    <p>Before accepting a presentation, validate issuer trust, the holder, credential status and time validity. Bind it to your service, current request and fresh challenge. Check the claimed result against the user's approved request. Source-level ZK circuits do not, by themselves, establish this whole chain of trust.</p>
    <h2>Handle consent and retention</h2>
    <p>Present who is asking, why, what will be shared and what will be retained. Provide refusal and alternative routes. Withdrawal blocks future use of that consent; previous lawful receipts follow the agreed retention policy. Do not promise that revocation erases every copy.</p>
    <h2>Select and test the actual interface</h2>
    <p>Agree the production credential profile and protocol with the issuer and services. Evaluate <a href="https://openid.net/specs/openid-4-verifiable-presentations-1_0.html">OpenID4VP</a> and <a href="https://openid.net/specs/openid-4-verifiable-credential-issuance-1_0.html">OpenID4VCI</a> where appropriate, then test independent implementations. No conformance or government recognition is established here.</p>
    <p>The <a href="https://docs.virtengine.com/veid/overview">VEID documentation</a> and <a href="https://github.com/virtengine/virtengine">source repository</a> provide engineering context. Pin any evaluated version and verify supported behaviour before creating production dependencies.</p>
    <p><a href="/for-services/pilot">Review the pilot proposal</a> · <a href="/for-services/issuer-policy">Review issuer trust</a></p>
  </div>
</Guide>
